window.docContents[227].resolve({"/#definitional-equality":{"contents":"In the definition of , there is a pattern case .\nThis applies in the length used in the first placeholder, so another way to write the underscore's type  is .\nSimilarly,  contains a pattern case .\nThis means that the type of the second underscore can be equivalently written Vect α (plusL n✝ k + 1).\n\nTo expose what is going on behind the scenes, the first step is to write the  arguments explicitly, which also results in daggerless error messages because the names are now written explicitly in the program:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAnnotating the underscores with the simplified versions of the types does not introduce a type error, which means that the types as written in the program are equivalent to the ones that Lean found on its own:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe first case demands a , and  has that type.\nThis is parallel to the way that appending the empty list to any other list returns that other list.\nRefining the definition with  instead of the first underscore yields a program with only one remaining underscore to be filled out:\n\n\n\n\n\nSomething very important has happened here.\nIn a context where Lean expected a , it received a .\nHowever,  is not an abbrev, so it may seem like it shouldn't be running during type checking.\nSomething else is happening.\n\nThe key to understanding what's going on is that Lean doesn't just expand abbrevs while type checking.\nIt can also perform computation while checking whether two types are equivalent to one another, such that any expression of one type can be used in a context that expects the other type.\nThis property is called definitional equality, and it is subtle.\n\nCertainly, two types that are written identically are considered to be definitionally equal— and  or  and  should be considered equal.\nAny two concrete types built from different datatypes are not equal, so  is not equal to .\nAdditionally, types that differ only by renaming internal names are equal, so  is the same as .\nBecause types can contain ordinary data, definitional equality must also describe when data are equal.\nUses of the same constructors are equal, so  equals  and  equals .\n\nTypes contain more than just function arrows, datatypes, and constructors, however.\nThey also contain variables and functions.\nDefinitional equality of variables is relatively simple: each variable is equal only to itself, so  is not definitionally equal to .\nFunctions, on the other hand, are more complicated.\nWhile mathematics considers two functions to be equal if they have identical input-output behavior, there is no efficient algorithm to check that, and the whole point of definitional equality is for Lean to check whether two types are interchangeable.\nInstead, Lean considers functions to be definitionally equal either when they are both fun-expressions with definitionally equal bodies.\nIn other words, two functions must use the same algorithm that calls the same helpers to be considered definitionally equal.\nThis is not typically very helpful, so definitional equality of functions is mostly used when the exact same defined function occurs in two types.\n\nWhen functions are called in a type, checking definitional equality may involve reducing the function call.\nThe type  is definitionally equal to the type  because  is definitionally equal to .\nTo check their equality, both are reduced to , and then the constructor rule can be used five times.\nDefinitional equality of functions applied to data can be checked first by seeing if they're already the same—there's no need to reduce  to check that it's equal to , after all.\nIf not, the function is called and replaced with its value, and the value can then be checked.\n\nNot all function arguments are concrete data.\nFor example, types may contain s that are not built from the  and  constructors.\nIn the type , the variable  is a , but it is impossible to know which  it is before the function is called.\nIndeed, the function may be called first with , and then later with , and then again with .\nAs seen in the definition of , variables with type  may also be passed to functions such as .\nIndeed, the type  is definitionally equal to the type .\n\nThe reason that  and  are definitionally equal is that 's pattern match examines its first argument.\nThis is problematic:  is not definitionally equal to , even though zero should be both a left and a right identity of addition.\nThis happens because pattern matching gets stuck when it encounters variables.\nUntil the actual value of  becomes known, there is no way to know which case of  should be selected.\n\nThe same issue appears with the  function in the query example.\nThe type  does not reduce to any datatype because the definition of  has separate cases for singleton lists and lists with at least two entries.\nIn other words, it gets stuck when trying to match the variable  against concrete  constructors.\nThis is why almost every function that takes apart or constructs a  needs to match the same three cases as  itself: getting it unstuck reveals concrete types that can be used for either pattern matching or constructors.\n\nThe missing case in  requires a Vect α (Nat.plusL n k + 1).\nThe + 1 in the index suggests that the next step is to use :\n\n\n\n\n\nA recursive call to  can construct a  with the desired length:\n\n\n\nNow that the program is finished, removing the explicit matching on  and  makes it easier to read and easier to call the function:\n\n\n\nComparing types using definitional equality means that everything involved in definitional equality, including the internals of function definitions, becomes part of the interface of programs that use dependent types and indexed families.\nExposing the internals of a function in a type means that refactoring the exposed program may cause programs that use it to no longer type check.\nIn particular, the fact that  is used in the type of  means that the definition of  cannot be replaced by the otherwise-equivalent .\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming with Dependent Types\u0009Pitfalls of Programming with Dependent Types","header":"7.5.1. Definitional Equality","id":"/#definitional-equality"}});